186485236} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2f8dbe-b7d6-5ec4-b856-e83b6f6604fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee2f8dbe-b7d6-5ec4-b856-e83b6f6604fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485322} :answer-field/id #uuid "91476299-4b29-5a42-b053-5f639a6f9a67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f188433-9891-58cb-8279-ce3757720a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-212962" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}}=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{27}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{27}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{27}}{\\sqrt{27}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{(\\sqrt{27})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{27} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that $27$ has a perfect square factor $(9).$ Therefore, we can rewrite $\\sqrt{27}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{27} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our original fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5\\cdot 3}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485515 :question/answer-fields [{:answ